                  ┌─────────────┐
                  │    START    │
                  └──────┬──────┘
                         ↓
               ┌──────────────────┐
               │ read(0, buf, 512)│
               └────────┬─────────┘
                        ↓
                   What is n?
                   /     |     \
                n=0     n<0    n>0
                 |       |       |
                 ↓       ↓       ↓
               STOP    ERROR   write(1,
                               buf, n)
                                  |
                                  ↓
                             success?
                              /    \
                            no      yes
                            ↓        |
                          ERROR      |
                                     |
                   ┌─────────────────┘
                   │
                   └────→ read again


cat comes from contatenation which here means
-> Read data from a file or standard input and write that data to standard output.

cat has no idea whether fd 0 is the keyboard, a file, 
or a pipe — and it has no idea whether fd 1 is the console, 
a file, or another program's input. It just reads from 0 and writes to 1. 
Because of the fd convention, this tiny loop works correctly no matter where 
input/output are actually connected. That's the whole point of the abstraction.


